-- title:
--   Proposition 5.14 on the choice graph — connecting nearby blocks under one global choice of majority cones
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$ and an angle $\vartheta$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, and let $r > 0$. Then there is a radius $R \ge r$ such that the following holds for every $\vartheta$-bounded configuration $\Gamma$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$ and for all scale parameters $h, \ell$ with $h > 0$, $\ell \ge 1$ subject to the *sparse population* condition
--   $$\frac{\sqrt{d}+1}{\sin(\vartheta/2)} \;<\; \frac{h}{\ell}.$$
--   Write $Q_\ell(u) = \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \bar A_\ell(u)$ for the *block* of lattice points in the closed cube of side $\ell$ centred at $u$, and $Q_\ell(hx)$ for the block indexed by $x$. Then there is a choice function $W$ assigning a double cone $W(B)$ to each subset $B \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ such that:
--
--   1. for every **finite** $B$, the cone $W(B)$ is *favored by majority* in $B$, i.e. the fibre $\{x \in B : \Gamma(x) = W(B)\}$ has maximal cardinality among the fibres of all double cones;
--   2. for all lattice points $z, x, y$ with $\|x-z\| \le r$ and $\|y-z\| \le r$, the blocks $Q_\ell(hx)$ and $Q_\ell(hy)$ are joined by a chain of blocks, all lying in the *town ball*
--   $$\{\,Q_\ell(hp) : p \in \mathbb{Z}^d,\ \|p - z\| < R\,\},$$
--   consecutive members $Q \ne P$ of which are adjacent in the sense that either every point of $P$ lies in $W(Q)$ based at every point of $Q$, or every point of $Q$ lies in $W(P)$ based at every point of $P$.
--
--   This is the renormalised connectivity statement: after replacing lattice points by blocks and each block's cones by a single majority cone, any two blocks with nearby indices are connected inside a ball whose radius $R$ depends only on $\vartheta$, $d$ and $r$ — not on the configuration, nor on the scales $h$ and $\ell$. Fixing one cone per block, rather than merely knowing a favored cone exists, is what allows a single representative point of a block to serve both of its incident edges.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Connectivity is the reflexive-transitive closure of the adjacency relation restricted to blocks of the town ball, so a chain of length zero is allowed and in particular $Q_\ell(hx)$ is connected to itself. The choice function $W$ is defined on all subsets of $\mathbb{R}^d$, but clause 1 constrains it only on finite ones; maximality of a fibre is expressed with extended cardinalities.
--
--   **Relation to the source.** This is a variant of **Proposition 5.14** of
--   Bux–Kassmann–Schulze on a graph the source does not define. Proposition 5.14 asserts
--   connectivity in Definition 5.13's *favored graph*, whose edge relation asks only that
--   *some* cone favored by majority in the source block do the job — the per-edge existential
--   that Remark 5.12 makes necessary, since the majority cone of a block need not be unique.
--   That statement is `QFS.renormalization`. Fixing one cone per block globally, as here,
--   gives a subgraph of it (`QFS.favoredAdj_of_choiceAdj`), so this conclusion is stronger;
--   but it also assumes $\ell \ge 1$, which the source does not, so neither statement implies
--   the other. The choice graph was introduced to carry a later step of Theorem 5.15's
--   construction, where one lattice representative per block has to serve both of the block's
--   incident edges.
